  "full_review": "Vin Diesel makes his bid for a spy-thriller franchise in the chaotic action adventure \"XXX\" (Columbia). The actor, propelled into the limelight after starring in \"The Fast and the Furious\" as a streetcar racer-turned-hijacker, returns as antisocial extreme-sports enthusiast, Xander Cage. Xander, or Triple X, a nickname he has tattooed on the back of his neck, makes a questionable living by selling Web videos of his thrill-seeking stunts.\n\n����������� Enter Augustus Gibbons (facially scarred Samuel L. Jackson), a veteran agent with the National Security Agency who recruits Xander for the shadowy world of espionage to fight a desperate and dangerous gang of Prague anarchists hankering to start a massive war using a biochemical-dispensing submarine. Xander is unwilling at first, but when Gibbons reminds him of his long rap sheet which could be cleared with this venture, Xander naturally agrees.\n\n����������� The tagline for the movie is \"A new breed of secret agent,\" but in fact the character and film, and its inevitable sequels, are an almost exact replica -- with a few modern tweaks -- of Cubby Broccoli's James Bond series. But while \"XXX\" has the outrageous stunts and cheeky dialogue similar to the 007 films, it has none of the polish. \"XXX\" even has its own Q character, albeit this one is a flip and sardonic MIT graduate. There is also the \"Bond girl\": the beautiful-but-cynical girlfriend (Italian actress Asia Argento) of the anarchists' leader (Maron Csokas). The death-defying stunts which show Diesel parachuting out of a stolen car as it plunges off a bridge or jumping over a burning house on a motorcycle are used to diminishing effect. After the third or fourth far-fetched feat, they no longer hold the same cachet.\n\n����������� Although it's mentioned only in passing, Gibbons makes a comment that the agency needs people like Xander because they are \"programmable and expendable.\" The film doesn't dwell on this thought, but its inclusion points to a casual disregard for life, which is troublesome.\n\n����������� As the extreme-sports criminal-turned-United-States-secret-agent, Diesel has some charm and charisma, but no emotional range. He delivers his lines in a flippant monotone that wears thin and he relies mainly on his buffed body to convey feeling.\n\n����������� Director Rob Cohen's testosterone-revved flick follows a halfhearted narrative with time-worn espionage elements. But Diesel's surprise success with \"The Fast and the Furious\" may guarantee the director a core male audience aged 18-24 who may be willing to forgive the film's many shortcomings for the vicarious thrill of snowboarding down a treacherous mountain with an avalanche fast approaching.\n\n����������� Due to an implied sexual encounter, recurring violent action sequences, brief drug content, fleeting rear nudity and some crass language, the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops classification is A-III -- adults. The Motion Picture Association of America rating is PG-13 -- parents are strongly cautioned that some material may be inappropriate for children under 13."
}